---
source: crates/luck_linter/src/fixtures.rs
---
warning: `while` loop body always returns, so the loop executes at most once
 --> Standalone:2:1
1 | local x = 1
2 | while x do return 1 end
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
help: if a single execution is intended, use a `do ... end` block

warning: numeric `for` loop body always breaks, so the loop executes at most once
 --> Standalone:1:1
1 | for i = 1, 10 do break end
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
help: if a single execution is intended, use a `do ... end` block

warning: `repeat` loop body always errors, so the loop executes at most once
 --> Standalone:1:1
1 | repeat error("x") until true
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
help: if a single execution is intended, use a `do ... end` block

warning: generic `for` loop body always returns, so the loop executes at most once
 --> Standalone:1:1
1 | for k, v in pairs(t) do return v end
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
help: if a single execution is intended, use a `do ... end` block

warning: `while` loop body always returns, so the loop executes at most once
 --> Standalone:2:1
1 | local c = 1
2 | while c do if c then return 1 else return 2 end end
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
help: if a single execution is intended, use a `do ... end` block

warning: `while` loop body always returns, so the loop executes at most once
 --> Standalone:1:1
1 | while true do for i = 1, 2 do break end return end
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
help: if a single execution is intended, use a `do ... end` block

warning: numeric `for` loop body always breaks, so the loop executes at most once
 --> Standalone:1:15
1 | while true do for i = 1, 2 do break end return end
  |               ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
help: if a single execution is intended, use a `do ... end` block

warning: numeric `for` loop body always exits, so the loop executes at most once
 --> Standalone:1:1
1 | for i = 1, 3 do if i then break else return end end
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
help: if a single execution is intended, use a `do ... end` block
